WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:05.106
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.707 --> 00:00:10.685
When I was a little girl,
my mom would hold each of my hands and

00:00:10.685 --> 00:00:13.660
lift me up as the waves
crashed over her feet.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.620
And as I grew up on the Jersey shore and
was big enough to stand on my own,

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:23.020
there was no water too cold, or
waves too rough to keep me from swimming.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:30.390
And to this day, the sound of waves
crashing brings a calm to my being.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.210
It sounds like home.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.489
Because like billions of people around
the world, I grew up near a coast.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.280
Sadly, we are changing our
coasts in dangerous ways.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.040
Our coasts are dynamic spaces.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.380
They divide the world in which we live
from the massive expanse of the ocean.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.780
Here, in California,
our coasts are rocky and cliffed.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.260
Think Half Moon Bay and along Route 1.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.400
But for
many miles of the world's coastlines,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:08.130
our water blends into land in
a special ecosystem known as wetlands.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.470
So what are wetlands?

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.450
Here water covers soil for
most of the year.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:18.105
And depending on where you're from,
they go by many names.

00:01:18.105 --> 00:01:23.500
They are mangroves and marshes,
they are swamps and slews.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.550
Today, I'll try to stick to wetlands for
short.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:31.430
And I want to show you that these
wetlands hold far more economic and

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.230
environmental power than you think.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.110
Wetlands protect us from the ocean
by buffering against storms,

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.590
and they protect the ocean from us
by filtering out our pollution.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:48.466
And so now you're thinking,
wetlands sound great.

00:01:48.466 --> 00:01:50.346
What's the problem?

00:01:50.346 --> 00:01:54.880
Over the past century, our wetlands
have disappeared at an alarming rate.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.100
Nearly half of the world's
wetlands are already gone.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.551
In the US, that's 220 million acres.

00:02:02.551 --> 00:02:05.589
Here in the Bay Area, we filled in and

00:02:05.589 --> 00:02:10.005
developed on top of 85%
of our historic wetlands.

00:02:10.005 --> 00:02:14.540
In other places we dredge our wetlands,
physically removing them to

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.996
create more water for fishing and
shipping or we dammed upstream and

00:02:18.996 --> 00:02:23.320
we starved our wetlands of the dirt and
salt they need to survive.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.837
So, how do we fix this?

00:02:26.837 --> 00:02:31.151
I'm here to convince you that,
whenever possible, we must not develop or

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:32.588
replace our wetlands and

00:02:32.588 --> 00:02:36.907
we need to get creative about restoring
the wetlands we've already lost.

00:02:36.907 --> 00:02:42.130
And to show you, let me share a couple
stories of the power of a salt marsh.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.141
It was the summer of 2012, and
I was living on the Chesapeake Bay,

00:02:47.141 --> 00:02:49.680
on the eastern shore of Virginia.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.540
One morning I was awoken by
a very particular, rotting smell.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.870
Imagine the worst smell you can fathom,

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.830
imagine that that smell
permeates your home, the air.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.060
So I walk to the beach.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.770
Just a couple hundred yards from my home.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:10.550
And as I approach the water,
that smell only got stronger.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:15.430
When I finally reached the bay,
there was no sand in sight,

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:20.780
only dead fish, as far as I can see,
in any direction.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:22.800
It was a fish kill.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.750
This horrible event became
a shockingly regular part of life

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.860
when I lived on the Chesapeake Bay.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.190
So let's start with the science.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.970
Farmers apply fertilizer to their
fields to increase production.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.260
While most of this fertilizer
is used by those plants,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.800
some of it washes into nearby streams and
eventually into bays and the ocean.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.440
Just like crops, phytoplankton,

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.780
the microscopic plants living in
the water, thrive on this new nutrients.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.040
These phytoplankton grow so
fast that they trigger a change

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.180
that leads to the loss of oxygen
in widespread areas of water.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.640
Just like us, fish breath oxygen.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.810
When fish reach these hypoxic zones,
areas without oxygen,

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:15.000
they die in large numbers and
wash up on shore.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.280
This is a crisis in coastal communities.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:21.790
It's not just an ecological blow.

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:24.150
This is an economic disaster for

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.720
all of the fishers who rely on
these fish for their income.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:35.010
But what if this fertilized
water never reached the ocean?

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:39.040
What if, instead, this fertilized
water went into wetlands, and

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.210
the plants there used all of the nutrients
and left none for the phytoplankton?

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.200
For many years,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.730
our coastal wetlands acted as this
natural filter between land and water.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.420
They absorbed the nutrients and

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.040
played this critical role
in our coastal communities.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:03.000
Sadly, with the loss of our wetlands,
these hypoxic zones,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.910
cover large regions of our world's
more fertile fishing grounds.

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:09.900
Including much of
the Gulf of Mexico today.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.820
Yet, this is a story of hope.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.190
In the Chesapeake Bay, we have seen
fish kills decreased from 50 kills

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:25.230
each year from hypoxia in the 80s,
to just ten today, with a management plan.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:29.048
Our coasts are resilient
when given a chance.

00:05:29.048 --> 00:05:32.970
So let's re-imagine communities
where fishers do not suffer at

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.270
the hands of farmers.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.340
And now you know the power of
a wetland to protect our ocean.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.090
Let's talk about the power
of a wetland to protect you.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.811
Climate change has transformed what
it means to live on our coast.

00:05:50.811 --> 00:05:54.585
Flooding is already the most
common natural disaster in the US.

00:05:54.585 --> 00:05:58.161
And it's only becoming more
common as sea levels rise and

00:05:58.161 --> 00:06:01.080
storms increase in frequency and
intensity.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:06.569
Those of us who live and
work on the coast are at the most risk.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:11.960
In the 20th century,
the Gulf Coast of the US was dredged for

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.400
expanded ports and
new shipping lanes, with

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.540
the belief that wetlands held little value
except breeding ground for mosquitos.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:23.456
We lost an average of 34
square miles each year.

00:06:23.456 --> 00:06:26.823
That's 16,000 football fields.

00:06:26.823 --> 00:06:32.822
It's bigger than Palo Alto, every year.

00:06:32.822 --> 00:06:37.439
Tragically, we were reminded of
the true value of our wetlands when

00:06:37.439 --> 00:06:41.499
Hurricane Katrina hit the city
of New Orleans in 2005.

00:06:41.499 --> 00:06:45.176
While Katrina was dangerous,
with or without wetlands,

00:06:45.176 --> 00:06:49.226
the absence of the natural defense
led to higher water levels and

00:06:49.226 --> 00:06:53.220
more flooding and likely more
property damage and lives lost.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:58.300
In an attempt to mitigate this risk,
we have hardened our coast.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:01.560
A process of replacing the natural
defenses of marshes and

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.170
sea grasses with seawalls and
the levies to separate land and sea.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:10.740
Concrete walls looks strong, but

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.600
with one major storm event like Katrina,
these walls are breached and

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:18.570
destroyed, taking with them the land
they were meant to protect.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:26.250
Nothing can guarantee safety or
permanence in the face of Mother Nature.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:30.070
Yet natural defenses can provide
a sustainable solution to questions of

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:31.220
coastal storm protection.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.800
Wetlands may not seem as
strong as concrete, but

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.650
researchers found that the presence
of mangroves in Florida reduced waves

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:46.040
caused by wind by 75%
during a storm surge.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:51.190
For every mile width of man growth
there was a decrease of nearly two and

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:54.750
a half feet of water level rise.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:59.130
For people of the Gulf Coast and for
people of coasts around the world,

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:03.520
wetlands really can mean
the difference between life and death.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:06.030
So now what?

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.250
Today, nearly half of
the world's population

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.510
lives within 100 miles of a coast.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:18.740
Eight of the world's ten largest
cities sit directly on the sea.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:24.649
We must protect our coastal wetlands
to protect our coastal communities.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:31.190
Thankfully, humans are brilliant
adoptable creatures.

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:32.470
And guess what?

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:34.580
Wetlands are resilient too.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.700
And look,
I'm not saying we never develop wetlands.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.500
I'm saying when we need to
make this difficult decision,

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:45.330
we consider the true value
of all wetlands provide us.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:49.750
And get creative about replacing all
of the natural defenses we'd lose.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:55.040
Because if we decide to protect our
coastal communities by restoring and

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.460
maintaining wetlands,
our intervention will turn the tide.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:06.320
So imagine a future where
we protect our coasts in

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.360
the face of storms and we protect our
ocean against negligence on land.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:17.500
Imagine a future where I have a coast
to enjoy the waves with my daughter.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:23.911
Because if we protect our coastal
wetlands, they protect us.

00:09:23.911 --> 00:09:30.690
And our coasts tell a story of
hope if only we choose to listen.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:31.409
Thank you.

00:09:31.409 --> 00:09:33.383
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:33.383 --> 00:09:36.379
[MUSIC]

